$(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-12\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186354406 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/1b/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360484 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st))(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s{cos}^{2}⁡(st)-s{sin}^{2}⁡(st) \\\\  & =s({cos}^{2}⁡(st)-{sin}^{2}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360556 "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}\\right)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362333 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$"] [17592186362524 "If $G=P\\,\\text{i}+Q\\,\\text{j}+R\\,\\text{k}$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $G$ all exist, then the divergence of $G$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot G=\\text{div}\\,G=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot G & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362533 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+(-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362536 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362542 "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362552 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362555 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365555 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))i-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))j+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))k \\\\  & =(3-4)i-(-1-8)j+(2+12)k \\\\  & =-1i+9j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366191 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))j+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =0\\cdot i-0\\cdot j+(5+\\sqrt{2})k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366198 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ x & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ x & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(-2+2)i-(-4+x)j+(8-2x)k \\\\  & =⟨0,4-x,8-2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(4-x)^{2}+(8-2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-8x+{x}^{2}+64-32x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+80 & =20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+60 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2,\\,6.$"] [17592186367659 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of 